<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aral Sea</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aral Sea is a large inland lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, between present-day Kazakhstan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, fed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> rivers. The steppe lands around it marked the northern fringes of the Achaemenid world. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz Turks</a> who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a> originated in the region near the lower Syr Darya and the Aral Sea.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aral-sea.jpg" alt="Aral Sea" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA), collage by Dylan240. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aral_Sea_1964_vs._August_25,_2026.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They never formed a single state but a constellation of tribes spread across the steppes and mountains from the <b>Aral Sea</b> to the Altai and the edges of the Tarim Basin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saka">Saka · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its origins lay in the Oghuz Turkic tribal confederation that had long occupied the lands north of the Caspian and <b>Aral Seas</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…of only two doubly landlocked countries in the world, the other being Liechtenstein: none of its rivers reaches the sea, and the <b>Aral Sea</b> on its northern edge, once the fourth-largest inland sea on Earth, has shrunk to about a tenth of its former area since the 1960s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Amu Darya delta near the <b>Aral Sea</b>, people whose remains resemble those of the nomads of the Andronovo culture took up irrigation farming, a group known as the Tazabagyab culture.</blockquote><a href="/culture/oxus-civilisation">Oxus civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>After Tauke Khan's death around 1718, the Kazakh Khanate split, with the Junior Juz holding the western steppe between the <b>Aral Sea</b>, the Caspian and the Ural River.</blockquote><a href="/state/junior-juz">Junior Juz</a></li><li><blockquote>The economy was based on irrigated cotton and rice, along with fishing on the <b>Aral Sea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/karakalpakstan">Karakalpakstan</a></li></ul></section>
